  "question_type": "multiple_choice",
  "question": "Six seats 1–6 in a row. A sits just left of B. C sits just right of B. D sits at an end. E sits next to D but not next to A. B sits in an even-numbered seat. F takes the remaining seat. Who sits in seat 4?",
  "options": [
   {
    "id": "A",
    "text": "A"
   },
   {
    "id": "B",
    "text": "B"
   },
   {
    "id": "C",
    "text": "C"
   },
   {
    "id": "D",
    "text": "F"
   }
  ],
  "answer": "D",
  "explanation": "A, B, C are consecutive in that order. Try D at seat 6: E = 5. A cannot be 4 or 6 (next to E=5 would be 4 or 6). Blocks avoiding E=5: 1-2-3 works (A=1, B=2 even ✓, C=3). Then F = 4. Check D at seat 1 instead: E = 2, A cannot be 1 or 3; blocks 3-4-5 give B=4 even but A=3 is next to E=2 ✗; blocks 4-5-6 give B=5 odd ✗. So the only solution is A1, B2, C3, F4, E5, D6. Seat 4 is F.",
  "reasoning": "Testing cases to find the unique seating.",
  "tags": [
   "p3",
   "high_ability",
   "logical",
   "ordering"
  ],
